{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Problem\n\nThe close gate's undeclared-paths refusal tells the author to declare the paths \"and retry\",\nwithout saying that declaring them is a **material plan change** which invalidates the\nplan-review attestation. The bare retry it appears to promise cannot succeed.\n\n`src/rebar/_commands/close_precheck.py:302-310`:\n\n```python\nundeclared = commit_impact.undeclared_paths(paths, impact, repo_root=code_root)\nif undeclared:\n    raise CommandError(\n        f\"Error: cannot close: commit {sha} changes \"\n        + \", \".join(undeclared)\n        + \", which the recorded file_impact does not declare. Declare them with \"\n        \"`rebar set-file-impact <ticket> ...` and retry \"\n        '(or override with --force=\"<reason>\").',\n```\n\nFollowing that instruction widens `file_impact`, which changes the material basis hash\n(`llm/plan_review/material_diff.py:110-155`), so the next close is refused as `stale-material`\n(`llm/plan_review/attest.py:582`). The author reads two refusals as a contradiction, when in fact\nboth are correct and the missing information is that a **re-review is the required next step**.\n\n## Scope: MESSAGE CLARITY ONLY — the gates are working as designed\n\nThis ticket originally proposed exempting additive `file_impact` changes from staling. **That was\ninvestigated and rejected**; see the RETRACTION comment for the full reasoning and citations. In\nshort:\n\n- `llm/plan_review/read_set.py:4` builds the attestation's scope \"from the ticket's declared\n  ``file_impact`` ∪ the files the review CITED\", so widening `file_impact` retroactively broadens\n  what an existing signature claims to cover.\n- `file_impact` is a direct review input at four sites (`pass1.py:242` prompt context,\n  `det_lint.py:267` `p9_file_impact_coverage`, `det_invariants.py:35`, `det_gate_rules.py`), so a\n  new path CAN introduce new findings.\n- `docs/plan-review-gate.md:1062` states the gate's purpose: \"`stale-material` — you attested and\n  then edited the plan — still blocks, **so the gate is not bypassable by editing after signing**.\"\n\n**No change to gate behaviour is in scope here.** Both refusals must keep firing exactly as they\ndo. The only defect is that the first message under-describes the remedy.\n\n## Direction\n\nExtend the message so it states three things instead of one: that the work went beyond the\nreviewed plan; that declaring the paths is a material change which will invalidate the attestation\n(`stale-material`); and that `rebar review-plan <ticket>` must be re-run before the close will\nsucceed. Keep the existing content — the exact undeclared paths and the `--force` escape hatch.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The undeclared-paths refusal names re-attestation as a required step: it says that widening\n      `file_impact` is a material plan change and that `rebar review-plan` must be re-run before\n      the close can succeed.\n- [x] The message still names the offending commit sha, the exact undeclared paths, the\n      `rebar set-file-impact` remedy, and the `--force=\"<reason>\"` override.\n- [x] A test asserts the message names the re-attestation requirement, and would FAIL against the\n      current wording — demonstrated by observing it RED before the change.\n- [x] No behavioural change to either gate: the undeclared-paths refusal and the `stale-material`\n      refusal fire under exactly the conditions they do today, verified by the existing gate tests\n      passing unmodified.\n- [x] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass; `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check`\n      reports `new=0 increased=0 stale=0`."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787255723782680001,"uuid":"ac31c20d-4cec-4f17-9935-fe8c5b9973d0"}